do we understand why the met has changed  its stance on the publication of sue  gray's report  no we don't and that's the question  that's really troubling people here in  westminster and beyond this morning  because it's not clear at all why on  tuesday the met police said that they  were happy for sue gray's report to be  published in full and yet just a few  days later we get this statement which  changes pretty much everything political  calculations the sugray report timeline  and of course what comes next in all of  that i'll just read you that met police  statement because it is worth having in  full they said this morning for the  events the met is investigating we asked  for minimal reference to be made in the  cabinet office report the met did not  ask for any limitations on other events  in the report or for the report to be  delayed but we have had ongoing contact  with the cabinet office including on the  content of the report to avoid any  prejudice to our investigation  now the met has actually been asked why  they have changed their stance and at  the moment they have no formal comment  to make on that they have indicated in  that statement that they believe there  could be potential prejudice but legal  experts will tell you that most of the  offences that they are said to be  investigating are so-called summary  offences which would only involve a fine  so it's very hard to see how that would  be prejudiced because of course there is  no jury trial there is a potential for  other things to be uncovered but i think  those waters are quite muddy there's  some confusion here about whether  actually this is more a communications  problem it's more a problem coming from  the met police perhaps they haven't  quite got themselves in order things  have moved very quickly here this week  of course we know that the sugray report  was expected earlier earlier on in the  week and the prime minister was due to  respond to that in the commons there was  a long day of waiting on wednesday when  it sounded like it may or may not come  i think the team in the cabinet office  behind the sugree report have been it's  fair to say quite frustrated by these  developments they are now making their  own calculation about what should happen  next do they publish something which  doesn't include those eight events we  don't know which events those relate to  and there is a worry that if they go  down that road then that might mean the  report doesn't have much of a bite to it  it might not fairly reflect what sugray  believes took place within downing  street and across whitehall so it might  not really do the job or she could wait  and hold back until the met finished  their investigation but that could be  weeks or even months and there is some  frustration not just from those in that  team but also beyond people want to see  this report it's been dragging on and on  and there is now a real sense actually  from politicians across the board that  what's happening just isn't good enough  we are starting to get some reaction  from those politicians nicola sturgeon  has said things are getting murky at by  the moment the liberal democrats  reflecting there could be an appearance  of an establishment stitch up that's the  strength of feeling and ian blackford  for the snp had this to say just moments  ago  parliamentarians we should be speaking  with a very clear voice this report must  be published it must be published in  full let's make sure that we hold this  government this prime minister to  account we get answers as to what was  going on this corrosive culture of  partying around number 10.  let's make sure that we hold this man to  account and i mean i'll say to  colleagues right across the house  particularly conservatives they have the  power to remove this man this can't go  on any longer this is an attempt to what  appears to be a whitewashing effort the  only one that wins as i've said is boris  johnson  there will be people out there who want  to know what number 10's involvement  potentially was in this situation  whether or not there was any leaning on  the metropolitan police potentially to  change the timing of this  there has been a question posed of the  prime minister's official spokesman this  morning at the lobby briefing that we  have for journalists that of course  happens every morning they have said  that they believe it is not the case  that there was any intervention by  number 10 with that process and that  it's for the cabinet office to liaise  with police but at the moment as you  asked me at the very start there what  why has there been this change from the  met police we just don't know and there  are many people now who suggest that  actually that is the key question that  needs answering today because it has  huge ramifications for the politics here  and for how things look whether or not  the met police is doing its job properly
 

